    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d612">

                <pb facs="0760" n="752" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg752"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.6363 Amiranian/51a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        Iran</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-04-27T13:00:00-04:00">April 27, 1938—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>28. To be decoded by Engert and to be divulged to no one: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Seaboard Oil Company has informed the Department that it has decided
                            to terminate its concessions in Iran and Afghanistan. Hart<anchor n="38"
                                xml:id="d612-f1"/> will proceed to Teheran and Clapp<anchor n="39"
                                xml:id="d612-f2"/> to Kabul within the next week or two from Baghdad
                            where they now are in order to give the two governments formal
                            notification of the decision. Inasmuch as the decision may cause adverse
                            effects, especially in Teheran, it will probably be necessary for you to
                            remain there until the situation becomes clarified. Bearing in mind the
                            difficulties which Dubois experienced in leaving Iran some years ago at
                            the time the Ulen concession was terminated,<anchor n="40"
                                xml:id="d612-f3"/> the Department desires you to consider possible
                            ways and means of assisting Amiranian’s American employees in departing
                            from the country in the event that the Iranian Government should attempt
                            to impede their movements.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>Referring to your 39, April 25, 1 p.m.,<anchor n="41" xml:id="d612-f4"
                            /> these developments will probably obviate the necessity of your
                            consulting at the Department and visiting Kabul. Pending further
                            developments, however, it will be necessary to ask you to defer all
                            plans for leave.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d612-f1" n="38" xml:id="d612-f1-content">
                <p>Charles C. Hart.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d612-f2" n="39" xml:id="d612-f2-content">
                <p>Frederick C. Clapp.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d612-f3" n="40" xml:id="d612-f3-content">
                <p>In October, 1932, Arthur W. Dubois, manager of Ulen &amp; Co., was refused an
                    exit visa by the Iranian Government unless he first transferred his power of
                    attorney to another representative. He was granted the visa after a strong
                    protest by the United States.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d612-f4" n="41" xml:id="d612-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
